# Git + Replit + GitHub Cheatsheet

This is a **minimal set of commands** to feel confident working in Replit while syncing to GitHub.

---

## 1. Everyday Rhythm

### Before you start coding
```bash
git pull


Get the latest changes from GitHub (teammates, CI, etc).

While you work (save a snapshot)
git status               # see what changed
git add -A               # stage all changes
git commit -m "message"  # save a snapshot with a clear message


Example:

git add -A
git commit -m "feat: add assignments gantt view"

When you’re ready to sync
git push


Sends your local commits to GitHub (origin main in this project).

2. Quick “What’s going on?” Commands

See current status (changes, branch, staged files):

git status


See recent commits (short view):

git log --oneline


Check what branch you’re on:

git branch --show-current


Check your GitHub remote:

git remote -v

3. Confirming If Your Changes Reached GitHub

From Replit shell:

git fetch origin main
git rev-parse --short HEAD
git rev-parse --short origin/main


If the two SHAs match → your local code and GitHub main are in sync.

If they differ → you probably need to git push.

4. Rollbacks (when things break)
4.1 Team-safe rollback (preferred)

Use when you want to undo bad changes, but keep history clean for everyone.

See recent commits:

git log --oneline


Pick a GOOD_COMMIT (a point where things worked).

Revert everything after that good commit:

git revert --no-commit <GOOD_COMMIT>..HEAD
git commit -m "Revert to <GOOD_COMMIT>"
git push


📌 This adds a new commit that undoes the bad stuff. It does not rewrite history.

4.2 Restore a single file from an older version

Use when only one file is messed up.

Find a commit where the file was good:

git log -- path/to/file


Restore the file from that commit:

git restore --source=<COMMIT> path/to/file
git commit -m "Restore path/to/file from <COMMIT>"
git push


Everything else in the repo stays as-is.

5. Replit + Checkpoints (optional)

If you enable Agent/Assistant in Replit:

Replit can create checkpoints (automatic milestones).

Each checkpoint becomes a local Git commit.

You still need to Push from the Git panel or shell:

git push


Restore flow:

Replit → Agent / History → choose a checkpoint → Restore.

Then run:

git add -A
git commit -m "restore: rollback to checkpoint"
git push

6. Minimal Muscle Memory

These are the 4 commands you’ll use the most:

git pull                               # update from GitHub
git status                             # see what's changed
git add -A                             # stage all changes
git commit -m "short, clear message"   # save a snapshot
git push                               # sync to GitHub


If you know these, you’re already in good shape. The rollback commands are there for when you need to undo something without panicking.


